<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · artifact</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dying Gaul</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Dying <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaul">Gaul</a> is an ancient marble statue of a wounded Celtic warrior, now in the Capitoline Museums in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. It is a Roman copy of a Hellenistic original, probably bronze, commissioned by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-i">Attalus I</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pergamon">Pergamon</a> in the late 3rd century BCE to celebrate his victories over the Galatians. The figure, naked but for a torc, sinks onto his shield, and is admired for its sympathetic portrayal of a defeated enemy.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dying-gaul.jpg" alt="Dying Gaul" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: User:Jean-Pol GRANDMONT This illustration was made by Jean-Pol GRANDMONT Please credit this : Jean-Pol GRANDMONT An e... · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:0_Galata_Morente_-_Musei_Capitolini_(1).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
